  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 132 AM MDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThe overall pattern in the long term has been consistent over the \npast few model runs: the high pressure remains situated near the \nFour Corners region, placing north to northeast flow over much of \nNM, and keeping the heat across western and northwestern NM. Daily \nthunderstorm chances will remain across the the same areas that have \nbeen seeing storms, albeit with varying coverages. The high looks\nto strengthen a decameter or two by the weekend, which would put \na limit on the storms that would develop across the high terrain. \nThus, PoPs are lowest on Saturday and Sunday (though still likely \nto see scattered afternoon thunderstorms). Guidance also continues\nto show moisture advection into the Desert Southwest by next \nweek, which looks to increase thunderstorm chances across central \nand western NM. This is because the high pressure looks to shift \nback further east allowing for more southerly flow on its western \nperiphery. However, ensemble clustering (and deterministic models)\nare differing on whether the high pressure continues to shift \neastward or wobble between the CO/NM and AZ/UT border, and also \nwhat the strength of the high will be. These differences will \nspell out where the best moisture will end up over the course of \nnext week. Regardless of the above uncertainty, the overall \nconsistency of consecutive model runs shows promise in more \nprecipitation returning to/continuing in areas across western and \nnorthern NM next week.",
